        "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x+{120}^{\u2218})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{\u2218}+{180}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{180}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{\u2218},{180}^{\u2218}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{\u2218}$",
        "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{120}^{\u2218} & ={30}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{120}^{\u2218} & ={330}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{\u2218}-{120}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={330}^{\u2218}-{120}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ 2x & =-{90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={210}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ x & =-{45}^{\u2218}+{180}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={105}^{\u2218}+{180}^{\u2218}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={105}^{\u2218}.$",
